WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:16.936
Marilla walked home one late April evening and realized happily that winter was over.

2
00:00:16.937 --> 00:00:23.068
She looked affectionately at Green Gables, and looked forward to a warm fire and a cup of tea with Anne.

3
00:00:23.069 --> 00:00:31.151
When she got home, the fire wasn't lit, and there was no sign of Anne anywhere. Marilla was disappointed and irritated.

4
00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:37.559
By the time Marilla got dinner ready, it was dark, and Anne had still not come home.

5
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:43.463
Marilla went upstairs and found Anne lying on the bed, face down among the pillows.

6
00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:49.157
"My goodness!" said the astonished Marilla. "Have you been asleep, Anne?"

7
00:00:49.158 --> 00:00:51.975
"No," was the muffled reply.

8
00:00:51.976 --> 00:00:57.613
"Are you sick then?" asked Marilla anxiously. Marilla moved closer to the bed.

9
00:00:57.614 --> 00:01:01.827
"No. But please, Marilla, go away and don't look at me.

10
00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:05.383
I'll never be able to go anywhere again."

11
00:01:05.384 --> 00:01:08.930
"Anne Shirley, what's the matter?" said Marilla.

12
00:01:08.931 --> 00:01:14.308
Anne turned over. "Look at my hair, Marilla," she whispered.

13
00:01:14.309 --> 00:01:20.459
Marilla lifted her candle and looked closely. Anne's hair certainly looked very strange.

14
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:29.095
"Anne, what have you done? Why, your hair is green!" And it was green.

15
00:01:29.096 --> 00:01:35.203
Never in all her life had Marilla seen anything so ugly as Anne's hair at that moment.

16
00:01:35.204 --> 00:01:42.012
"Yes, it's green," moaned Anne. "I thought nothing could be as bad as red hair.

17
00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:46.608
But now I know it's ten times worse to have green hair."

18
00:01:46.609 --> 00:01:57.495
"Anne," Marilla said, "I've been expecting something to happen because you haven't done anything strange for two months! How did your hair turn green?"

19
00:01:57.496 --> 00:01:58.936
"I dyed it."

20
00:01:58.937 --> 00:02:04.856
"Dyed it? You dyed your hair?" Marilla looked at Anne sternly.

21
00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:13.308
"Well," continued Marilla sarcastically, "I would have dyed it a decent color, at least. I wouldn't have dyed it green."

22
00:02:13.309 --> 00:02:19.359
"He said it would turn my hair a beautiful raven black," protested Anne.

23
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.960
"Who said that?"

24
00:02:20.961 --> 00:02:26.215
"The peddler that was here this afternoon. I bought the dye from him."

25
00:02:26.216 --> 00:02:30.311
"Anne Shirley! This is what your vanity has done.

26
00:02:30.312 --> 00:02:34.057
Now give your hair a good wash," Marilla said.

27
00:02:34.058 --> 00:02:39.927
Anne washed her hair, but it made no difference. "Oh Marilla, what can I do?

28
00:02:39.928 --> 00:02:51.945
People have forgotten my other mistakes—shouting at Mrs. Lynde, making Diana drunk, jumping on Miss Barry, and cracking a slate on Gilbert Blythe.

29
00:02:51.946 --> 00:02:55.109
But they'll never forget this one."

30
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.901
For the next week, Anne went nowhere and washed her hair every day.

31
00:02:59.902 --> 00:03:07.045
At the end of the week, Marilla said firmly, "It's no use, Anne. Your hair must be cut off."

32
00:03:07.046 --> 00:03:09.067
Anne nodded and wept.

33
00:03:09.068 --> 00:03:13.650
Marilla's haircut was very thorough, and the result was not pretty.

34
00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:18.246
Anne's hair was quite short, and it made a sensation in school on Monday.

35
00:03:18.247 --> 00:03:22.970
But to her relief, nobody guessed the real reason behind her haircut.

36
00:03:22.971 --> 00:03:27.329
However, Josie Pye did tell Anne she looked like a scarecrow.

37
00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.844
A few weeks later, the girls were standing at the brook.

38
00:03:30.845 --> 00:03:36.293
In the brook was a small boat. It was Anne's idea that they act out a poem.

39
00:03:36.294 --> 00:03:39.907
"You must be the Lily Maid, Anne," said Diana.

40
00:03:39.908 --> 00:03:42.909
"I'm not brave enough to float down the river."

41
00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:48.374
"But it's ridiculous to have a redheaded Lily Maid," said Anne.

42
00:03:48.375 --> 00:03:53.138
"Your hair is much darker since you cut it," said Diana earnestly.

43
00:03:53.139 --> 00:04:00.201
"Really?" exclaimed Anne, flushing with delight. Then she went back to deciding the parts for their skit.

44
00:04:00.202 --> 00:04:03.812
"I'll be the Lily Maid," she agreed reluctantly.

45
00:04:03.813 --> 00:04:08.393
"Ruby, you be King Arthur, and Diana must be Lancelot."

46
00:04:08.394 --> 00:04:14.139
Anne lay down in the boat, closed her eyes and folded her hands on her chest.

47
00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:17.950
"She really looks dead," whispered Ruby Gillis.

48
00:04:17.951 --> 00:04:22.789
Instead of lilies Diana placed a posy of violets in Anne's hands.

49
00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:29.775
"Now she's ready," said Ruby. The girls kissed the "Lily Maid" and pushed the boat off.

50
00:04:29.776 --> 00:04:35.458
For a few minutes, as the boat slowly drifted, Anne enjoyed the romance of her journey.

51
00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:40.857
Then something very unromantic happened. The boat began to leak.

52
00:04:40.858 --> 00:04:46.619
The Lily Maid was forced to get up and look at the big crack, through which water was pouring.

53
00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:51.368
Anne gave a gasping little scream that nobody heard, and then she went pale.

54
00:04:51.369 --> 00:04:55.941
But she stayed calm. There was one chance—just one.

55
00:04:55.942 --> 00:05:01.670
Anne prayed that the boat would keep drifting toward the bridge and float near one of its columns.

56
00:05:01.671 --> 00:05:06.486
The columns were made of old tree trunks, with branch stubs that she could sit on.

57
00:05:06.487 --> 00:05:13.197
Anne's prayer was answered. The boat drifted under the bridge, and Anne scrambled onto a column.

58
00:05:13.198 --> 00:05:17.009
She sat there, with no way of getting up or down.

59
00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.839
The boat kept drifting and then sank.

60
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:25.612
Ruby and Diana waited farther down the brook, and they saw the boat disappear underwater.

61
00:05:25.613 --> 00:05:30.041
They went white as sheets, shrieked, and ran off.

62
00:05:30.042 --> 00:05:35.785
For the unlucky Lily Maid, the minutes ticked by, each one like an hour.

63
00:05:35.786 --> 00:05:41.515
Why didn't someone come? Where had her friends gone? Suppose nobody came!

64
00:05:41.516 --> 00:05:47.193
Just when Anne thought she could hold on no longer, Gilbert Blythe rowed toward her.

65
00:05:47.194 --> 00:05:53.360
"Anne Shirley! How did you get there?" he exclaimed. He helped Anne into his boat.

66
00:05:53.361 --> 00:06:00.050
She sat there, furious and soaking wet. "What happened?" asked Gilbert.

67
00:06:00.051 --> 00:06:05.978
"We were playing, and I was the Lily Maid," Anne said, not even looking at Gilbert.

68
00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:13.133
"I had to float to Camelot—I mean, down the brook. The boat leaked, and I had to get out.

69
00:06:13.134 --> 00:06:16.476
Can you row me to the bank?" asked Anne.

70
00:06:16.477 --> 00:06:18.184
Gilbert obliged.

71
00:06:18.185 --> 00:06:22.504
"I'm very grateful," said Anne haughtily.

72
00:06:22.505 --> 00:06:26.605
"Anne," he said quickly, "I'm sorry I made fun of your hair.

73
00:06:26.606 --> 00:06:32.257
It was only a joke. Besides, it was so long ago. Can't we be friends?"

74
00:06:32.258 --> 00:06:37.619
For a moment Anne hesitated. Her heart gave a strange, quick little beat.

75
00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:42.861
But then the scene of two years ago flashed in her head, like it had happened yesterday.

76
00:06:42.862 --> 00:06:47.251
She hated Gilbert Blythe! She would never forgive him!

77
00:06:47.252 --> 00:06:52.895
"No," she said coldly. "I will never be friends with you, Gilbert Blythe."

78
00:06:52.896 --> 00:06:57.433
"All right!" Gilbert went down to his boat, his cheeks red.

79
00:06:57.434 --> 00:07:00.834
"I'll never ask you to be friends again, Anne Shirley.

80
00:07:00.835 --> 00:07:04.881
And I don't care either!" He rowed away furiously.

81
00:07:04.882 --> 00:07:19.130
As Anne walked home, she held her head high, but she was conscious of an odd feeling—regret.
